WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.240
I have no idea how to make 
a funny introduction for this video.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:07.700
So instead, I'm going to give you a quick slide show 
of cool awesome Slavic stuff. Go!

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.600
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.220
Today I'm going to talk about the Slavic languages.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.620
They may not get as much attention as 
the Romance languages and the Germanic languages,

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:34.720
but the Slavic languages are an important family 
of languages with 315 million native speakers

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.580
and covering over half the area of Europe.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:45.040
That includes Eastern Europe, the Balkans, 
as well as the European part of Russia and 
of course the Asian part of Russia as well.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.980
Slavic languages are a branch 
of the Indo-European language family.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.400
If we go all the way back to the beginning, then
there was a language called Proto-Indo-European,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.040
which is the language from which 
all Indo-European languages descended.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:05.140
That includes the Romance languages,
the Germanic languages, the Indo-Iranian languages 
and, of course, the Slavic languages as well as others.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:10.420
One language that developed from Proto-Indo-European 
was Proto-Balto-Slavic.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:15.060
That language is the ancestor of both 
the Baltic languages and the Slavic languages.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:22.040
But the Slavic language family broke off 
from Proto-Balto-Slavic around 1500 to 1000 BCE

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:29.020
The language then underwent a 2000 year period of
gradual development but, during that time, it remained 
a single unified language.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:37.100
Around 500 CE, there was a language called Proto-Slavic
that would later split and would give birth
to all of the modern Slavic languages of today.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.580
Proto-Slavic was the ancestor of all Slavic Languages.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.500
The time between 500 CE and 1000 CE
was called the Common Slavic Period.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.800
And during this period Proto-Slavic 
began to diverge into dialects.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.060
And, as Slavic people migrated over a wider area,
those differences increased.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:02:00.900
And, by about 1,000 CE, there were distinct West-Slavic, East-Slavic and South-Slavic Languages.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:05.540
Those three languages would later become 
three branches of the Slavic language family.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.020
And the dialects of those languages would become 
the different languages in each branch.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:17.260
The East-Slavic languages include :  Russian, Ukrainian 
and Belarusian which is sometimes called "White-Russian".

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:24.260
The South Slavic language family includes Serbo-Croatian, 
Bulgarian and Macedonia which are very similar,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.020
Slovenian and also the liturgical language known 
as Old Church Slavonic.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.120
"Slavonic" just means "Slavic".

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:38.460
The West Slavic languages include Polish, Czech, Slovak, 
Sorbian which is a minority language in eastern Germany,

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.980
Kashubian, which is a minority language in Poland.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:48.340
The Slavic languages are much closer to each other than 
the languages of other Indo-European language families.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:55.940
That's because Proto-Slavic split into different languages 
relatively recently, compared to other language families 
like Germanic and Romance.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.780
Some people claim that all Slavic languages are 
mutually intelligible to a large degree, 
but that's not really true.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:13.220
But, from what I understand, the speaker of any Slavic 
language can probably find a way to make some sort of 
simple conversation with the speaker of any other Slavic 
language or most other Slavic languages.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:19.280
That doesn't mean they can understand everything 
but that means when they're talking face-to-face, 
maybe they can find a way to communicate.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:25.200
And, within each of those three branches
of the language family, there seems to be
 quite a lot of mutual intelligibility.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.360
For example, the East-Slavic branch. 
Here are some average figures from native speaker's 
statements on the internet.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:40.120
So, for Russian-speakers, Belarusian is about 
74% intelligible in its spoken form
and 85% intelligible in its written form.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:46.800
Ukrainian is 50% intelligible in it's spoken form 
and 85% intelligible and it's written form.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:54.600
But, in both cases, there are also transitional dialects. 
For example, Ukrainian is more similar to the 
Russian dialects in the south of the country,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.280
rather than the standard language 
which is based on dialects of the north.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:03.820
So speakers of those southern Russian dialects 
might have an easier time communicating 
with someone from Ukraine.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.940
Here's another example from a Langfocus viewer, 
named "anointedfighter".

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:14.960
He says "I'm a native Bulgarian-speaker, 
I can understand 80% Serbian and 80%  Russian, 
especially if they are written.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.620
Czech Slovak and Slovenian, I can get 60% 
and, from Polish, only 40%.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.000
Bulgarian and Serbian are both part of the South Slavic branch,
so he can understand 80% of Serbian.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:30.680
But he can also understand 80% of Russian, 
even though it's in the East Slavic branch.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:36.660
Slavic was still a single unified language in the 9th c.
when the first known Slavic writings were written.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:41.280
These writings were written in Old Church Slavonic. 
Slavonic just means Slavic, remember.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.620
It was the first Slavic literary language and it was 
standardized by the Saints Cyril and Methodius.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:55.080
So that they could translate the Bible and other religious works 
into the Slavic language and they could minister 
to the Slavs that convert them to Christianity.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:59.840
At this point, there were Slavic dialects
but they were still relatively close to each other. So...

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.460
Old Church Slavonic was widely understood

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:08.140
Old Church Slavonic went on to become the language 
of religious ceremony for the Eastern Orthodox Church

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:12.240
and the Eastern Catholic Church 
and it's still used by many churches today.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:20.780
One of the writing systems Cyril and Methodius used 
to write Old Church Slavonic was the "cyrillic script",
for the word cyrillic comes from the saint's name, Cyril.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:30.340
The cyrillic script was adapted from the old Greek uncial script,
with some additional letters to represent sounds 
found in Church Slavonic that were not found in Greek.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:36.900
Today, there are Slavic languages written in the cyrillic script 
and there are also those written in variations of the Latin script.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:43.700
Those written in the cyrillic script tend to be 
for cultures  in which the Eastern Orthodox 
or Eastern Catholic Church are dominant.

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.700
Those written in the Latin script tend to be for cultures 
in which the Roman Catholic Church is dominant.

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:58.760
One of the common features of Slavic languages,
aside from Bulgaria and Macedonian, is that 
the nouns and also pronouns are highly inflected
and have many grammatical cases.

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:04.540
Grammatical case means that the noun or the pronoun 
changes form depending on its function in the sentence.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:07.660
In English, the only example we have of this 
is with the pronouns.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:12.020
For example, "I" is the subject form 
and "me" is the object form.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:16.620
But nouns can have multiple forms like this as well, 
if they have grammatical cases.

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.980
Slavic languages have up 
to seven different grammatical cases.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.280
Let's look at an example for the Polish word for "bottle".

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:33.380
So the word "bottle" changes form depending on 
which case it's being used.

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:38.240
I won't go through each one and say which case it is 
but you can see that the form of the noun changes.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:46.300
For people whose native language is not a highly 
inflected language with grammatical cases like that,
this can make learning a Slavic language a challenge.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:52.960
You not only have to memorize those different forms 
but, when you're speaking, you have to constantly
be aware of which form the word should be ,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.180
which function the word is fulfilling in the sentence.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:01.920
Another characteristic of Slavic languages 
is that they don't have articles,
that means like "the" or "a" in English.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:06.200
And again, the exception is Bulgarian 
and Macedonian for some reason.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:11.740
The lack of grammatical cases and 
the presence of articles might make Bulgarian 
and Macedonian easier to learn,

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:14.920
but the most widely studied Slavic language is Russian

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:20.540
And that's probably because it's the most widely spoken. 
There are 150 million native speakers of Russian.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:25.440
and, if we include second language speakers, 
there are 260 million proficient speakers.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:30.160
The second most widely spoken is Polish,
with around 40 million native speakers.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.360
The third most widely spoken is Ukrainian,
 with about 30 million speakers.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:37.900
The fourth is Serbo-Croatian with about 19 million speakers.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:44.460
And yes, I know some people consider those to be 
separate languages but I will leave that topic
for a different video, for now.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.100
The fifth is Czech with 10 million people.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.740
And, of course, there are numerous other
important Slavic languages too.

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:56.140
Really there's so much to say about the various 
different Slavic languages that I can't talk
 about it all in one video.

00:07:56.140 --> 00:08:00.880
But, please, stay tuned for future videos 
in which I talk about specific Slavic languages.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:06.180
That brings us to the end of this video but 
the question of the day for Slavic language speakers is:

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:11.200
Can you communicate with the speakers
of different Slavic languages and if so which ones?

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:15.280
Is it just the ones in your language branch,
 like East-Slavic or South Slavic?

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:18.920
Or can you communicate with the speakers 
in other branches as well?

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.940
Let us know in the comments down below.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.100
And other people you can comment 
about whatever you want.

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:27.460
Alright, thank you for watching and have a nice day!

